                  <origin>
                     <origDate when-custom="110X/111X" notBefore="1100" notAfter="1119" calendar="#gregorian">s. xii in</origDate>
                  </origin>
                  <provenance type="#e s"><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_469">Rochester, Kent.
                        Cathedral priory of St Andrew</orgName><p>Erasure at foot of fol. 1 where
                        the Rochester inscription should be; it was no doubt (letters in italics are
                        legible under UV): 'Liber de claustro Roffense per J . . . . monachum'. The
                        script is partly of a Rochester type and the binding is similar to another
                        Rochester manuscript, Bodley 134, and like that has the words 'S Wigorniae'
                        on one of the endleaves (s. xvi). There is also at fol. ii a medieval
                        contents list identifiable with the medieval catalogue
                     entry.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Said to have been given to Bodley by the Dean
                     and Canons of Windsor in 1612; see under Windsor also.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">There is not any evidence or probability that
                     this manuscript was once at Pembroke, Cambridge, as guessed by M. R.
                     James.</provenance>
               </history>
